WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.199 --> 00:00:03.660
So let's begin with UWidget.

2
00:00:03.660 --> 00:00:07.259
This is the perfect start to looking into your Unreal Motion Graphics as UWidget lies

3
00:00:07.259 --> 00:00:08.259
at the base of it.

4
00:00:08.259 --> 00:00:12.039
But before we start, it's important to understand what Unreal Motion Graphics actually

5
00:00:12.039 --> 00:00:13.099
is.

6
00:00:13.099 --> 00:00:17.640
UMG gives developers a framework that wraps around Slate, which is Unreal's custom

7
00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:19.579
UI programming framework.

8
00:00:19.579 --> 00:00:23.739
The editor UI is actually largely built on top of this exact framework.

9
00:00:23.739 --> 00:00:28.079
It also provides a visual UI authoring tool that allows developers to create UI

10
00:00:28.079 --> 00:00:31.160
elements in an easy way that is quick to pick up.

11
00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:35.399
So with that in mind, let's take a look at what UWidget is.

12
00:00:35.399 --> 00:00:39.159
Essentially UWidget is the base class that developers can access within native, and this

13
00:00:39.159 --> 00:00:44.189
is important to note as UWidget is not a type that can be easily created within blueprint.

14
00:00:44.189 --> 00:00:48.770
It serves as the base implementation of a widget in native, and the class itself introduces

15
00:00:48.770 --> 00:00:53.520
some of the base functionality that developers can use when creating their own UI elements.

16
00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:56.960
As you may have noticed from the prefix of this type, this class is part of the reflection

17
00:00:56.960 --> 00:00:58.219
system.

18
00:00:58.219 --> 00:01:02.719
It's based from the UVisual class which in turn inherits from the UObject class, so we

19
00:01:02.719 --> 00:01:07.879
can think of the UWidget as a UObject wrapper for a Slate widget.

20
00:01:07.879 --> 00:01:11.540
The reason why we say UWidget is at the base of UMG is because some of the more

21
00:01:11.540 --> 00:01:15.680
common UI elements that you're likely to use when creating your own UI is based

22
00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:20.359
from this class, such as the text box, the overlay, the horizontal box and the vertical

23
00:01:20.359 --> 00:01:21.620
box.

24
00:01:21.620 --> 00:01:26.859
UWidget, and all types deriving from it, essentially lie on top of Slate.

25
00:01:26.859 --> 00:01:31.400
As we've discussed already, UWidgets act as a wrapper for those Slate widgets.

26
00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:35.680
More importantly, UWidgets provide us engineers with an almost familiar coding environment.

27
00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:39.700
Much of the same principles of working with UObjects also apply here when working

28
00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:41.090
with UWidget.

29
00:01:41.090 --> 00:01:45.750
When creating a UWidget, in the backend we create an SWidget that will be the UI element

30
00:01:45.750 --> 00:01:48.150
that we actually end up rendering to the screen.

31
00:01:48.150 --> 00:01:54.459
Lastly, it's also crucial to understand that UMG widgets exist solely on the client.

32
00:01:54.459 --> 00:01:57.959
Now that we've got a brief understanding of the bedrock of the Unreal Motion Graphics

33
00:01:57.959 --> 00:02:01.280
framework, let's take a look at the main class that we're likely to be working with

34
00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:05.459
when authoring our own UI, that being the UserWidget.

35
00:02:05.459 --> 00:02:09.659
The UserWidget is the first usable class for all Unreal widgets.

36
00:02:09.659 --> 00:02:13.819
It's the default class that the UMG Blueprint editor initialises and is essentially the

37
00:02:13.819 --> 00:02:17.449
UObject of the UMG world.

38
00:02:17.449 --> 00:02:22.009
While it does extend off of UWidget, as engineers, it really is with the UserWidget that we'll

39
00:02:22.009 --> 00:02:24.650
start developing our own UI implementations.

40
00:02:24.650 --> 00:02:28.430
We'll be extending off of UserWidget in C++ to build our own custom elements for the

41
00:02:28.469 --> 00:02:29.789
most part.

42
00:02:29.789 --> 00:02:34.270
They can be created at runtime and on demand with a templated create widget call, and

43
00:02:34.270 --> 00:02:38.469
the syntax here is almost identical to the new object syntax, with some const expressions

44
00:02:38.469 --> 00:02:41.430
under the hood to enforce sensible outers.

45
00:02:41.430 --> 00:02:44.590
For instance, we can't have any random actor own the widget.

46
00:02:44.590 --> 00:02:48.870
The compiler will ensure that the outer is either another widget, a widget tree,

47
00:02:48.870 --> 00:02:51.990
which is essentially a data structure keeping track of how widgets are nested within

48
00:02:51.990 --> 00:02:57.830
each other, it needs to be a player controller, the game instance, or the game world.

49
00:02:57.870 --> 00:03:02.229
Unlike UObject, UserWidgets keep track of the game world and the local player controller,

50
00:03:02.229 --> 00:03:06.030
meaning we can pull meaningful information at runtime, very useful for stuff like health

51
00:03:06.030 --> 00:03:09.389
bars that need access to information about the local player.

52
00:03:09.389 --> 00:03:12.590
The UserWidget also has some critical functions publicly available.

53
00:03:12.590 --> 00:03:16.710
Firstly, we've got the add to viewport, and this does exactly as you'd expect.

54
00:03:16.710 --> 00:03:21.090
It adds this widget to the local player's viewport and takes in a Z order as a parameter.

55
00:03:21.090 --> 00:03:24.349
If we've got two widgets occupying the same space, one of them has obviously got

56
00:03:24.349 --> 00:03:29.060
to occlude the other one, and the Z order is what drives these fake depths on the screen.

57
00:03:29.060 --> 00:03:32.500
The higher the Z order, the higher priority is given on the screen.

58
00:03:32.500 --> 00:03:36.939
In other words, the lower the Z order, the further into the scene the widget will be rendered.

59
00:03:36.939 --> 00:03:39.939
After that, we've got add to screen, and this is similar to add to viewport, but

60
00:03:39.939 --> 00:03:41.500
with one key difference.

61
00:03:41.500 --> 00:03:44.780
The concept of a viewport is married to the concept of a local player.

62
00:03:44.780 --> 00:03:49.990
This may not be an issue for 99% of use cases, as you'll pretty much always have a local player.

63
00:03:49.990 --> 00:03:53.469
However, in cases where the local player is yet to be initialised, we may want

64
00:03:53.509 --> 00:03:56.110
to add it directly to the screen's canvas.

65
00:03:56.110 --> 00:04:01.960
Add to screen will add the widget to the canvas without even attempting to poll for a local player's viewport.

66
00:04:01.960 --> 00:04:06.159
From there, we've got remove from viewport, and again, this does exactly as you'd expect.

67
00:04:06.159 --> 00:04:10.090
It will remove this widget from the viewport regardless of its container.

68
00:04:10.090 --> 00:04:13.530
Finally, we've got remove from parent, and it's a bit more of a bespoke function

69
00:04:13.530 --> 00:04:15.169
than remove from viewport.

70
00:04:15.169 --> 00:04:18.610
Say we've got an inventory screen filled with many inventory slots.

71
00:04:18.610 --> 00:04:22.769
Calling remove from parent on the inventory slot will remove it from the overall inventory

72
00:04:22.769 --> 00:04:26.170
screen container, without necessarily removing it from the viewport.
